WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:17.910
Welcome to Now You See It.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.980
One of the most important characters in a movie often times is actually the setting where the movie takes place.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.720
In the same way that a character has a personality, influences the plot or represents a certain idea,

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.300
so too can a setting just as effectively contribute to the mood, style or themes in a story.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.060
While settings always indicate time or place,

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.820
I want to look at the many instances when setting goes even deeper and either gives insight into a character,

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:45.000
symbolizes a certain idea, moves forward the plot, or establishes mood.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:50.780
A setting is wherever a scene takes place and can be anywhere from space to a desert to virtual reality.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.120
A lot of the time, a setting is an extension of a character.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.700
Depending on where a character works, lives or spends their free time, we learn more about them.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:08.700
Perhaps the best example of the use of setting to describe a character is the Xanadu in Citizen Kane.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:13.740
This castle where Charles Kane lives is ridiculously large, elusive, secluded and creepy.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.240
The inside of his mansion has many rooms; some of them almost completely empty,

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.860
others filled to the brim with knick knacks and other trophies Kane cares nothing about.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.540
His home seems either barren or artificially stuffed with objects.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:29.060
For Kane to live there, we infer that he's extremely wealthy looking to separate himself from the world,

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:33.880
lonely, empty and unsuccessfully filling his emptiness with pointless trophies.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:40.040
This setting contrasts nicely with the small, cozy cabin he lived in as a child, which was much more intimate at home.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.880
About Schmidt has a similar use of setting.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:48.080
If a character is introduced in a completely barren room, what does that say about the character?

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:53.740
In Lost in Translation, Charlotte and Bob feel confused and out of place in Tokyo

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:57.180
and the setting reinforces how they feel out of touch with their significant others.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.220
As they grow closer and understand how to cope with their fears and problems,

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.820
the portrayal of Tokyo goes from dark and confusing close ups at the beginning

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.040
to clear bright wide shots at the end.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.680
The settings showcased their growth as characters.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:12.600
A setting can also be a strong symbol in the movie and represent some idea.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.860
For example, Vietnam war movies usually portray the war as ambiguous, senseless and full of confusion,

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.860
and these ideas are represented in the dense, dark and menacing jungle where it's oftentimes hard to tell friend from foe.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.460
In Little Miss Sunshine, the beauty pageant represents a flawed American Dream.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.100
Beauty pageants are a strange phenomenon in American society,

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.540
where little girls are paraded onstage and judged on their outer appearance.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.640
The film uses the setting of the beauty pageant

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.980
to critique how the American dream is composed of superficial and unfulfilled goals.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.300
The pageant is a representation of those who buy into this false dream.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:47.380
The family members rejecting the beauty pageant and defining their own happiness themselves

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:52.000
conveys the message that one's worth is determined by the self and not by a panel of judges.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.780
A similar example would be Office Space,

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:59.340
where his office represents a world where people put financial security at a well-paying job over their own happiness.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:00.940
A setting can really represent anything.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.460
In Life of Pi, Pi's journey across the ocean is a representation of his spiritual journey.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:08.420
While the meaning of this setting is more strongly emphasized in the novel,

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:14.020
the film still makes the connection between his spiritual journey across the ocean and a person's spiritual Journey in life.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.800
One important part of his journey is landing on the moving island.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.900
While he can survive on this island, the island is toxic and he would live his life trapped on it

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.840
without completing his journey and dying alone and unfulfilled.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:28.600
The island is a symbol of the choice everyone has to avoid completing his or her spiritual journey.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:30.860
The movie criticizes staying on this island.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.540
The setting of the island represents an obstacle in every person's spiritual journey.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.120
If someone wants they can never figure out what they believe in

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.600
and instead remain on the island and die unfulfilled and forgotten.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.780
The movie criticizes this religious choice and uses the setting to convey this criticism.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.740
"I saw how my life would end if I stayed on that island."

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.220
"Alone, forgotten."

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.740
"I had to get back to the world or die trying."

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:59.020
One important thing to note when analyzing a setting is what ends up happening to it.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.000
Do characters leave it or do they stay?

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:04.120
The most powerful ending of a setting is when it gets blown up or burned down

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:07.980
because that represents a rejection of whatever that setting symbolizes.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.600
Settings can act like characters when they influence the plot.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:13.980
Settings have the power to leave people stranded on a desert island, kill them,

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.040
or force two characters to come together.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:18.960
"Look, we're moving!"

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:23.180
Characters can never truly remove themselves from setting

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:26.560
and setting continuously gets used to advance the story forward.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:30.900
Not every setting has a higher meaning but just about each one sets a certain mood for a scene.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.000
Film noir sets the mood of mystery and deception

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.640
by placing characters in settings with loss of shadow lighting and contrast.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.460
Another very common setting trope is menacing lightning

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:42.840
which consistently conveys fear or doom, often in a comedic way.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.680
Filmmakers really have limitless options in how a setting conveys a mood.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.120
If someone is on a setting high up, that shows power.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:58.420
If someone is set low in the dirt, it signifies a lack of power.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:04.480
Whether a setting is big, cramped, clean, dirty, safe or dangerous,

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.820
it can contribute infinite layers to a story.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:08.420
Setting is an underrated character.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:12.960
Settings provide just as much meaning, symbolism, plot, elements and mood as a real-life character.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:18.060
So when analyzing a scene, don't forget that just because settings are in the background doesn't mean they're not worth noticing.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:19.340
Thanks for watching.

